    "q-138749": {
      "dom_id": "step-q138749",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Consider the function $y=6{x}^{x}.$ Its derivative is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=6{x}^{x}\\cdot g(x).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(x).$",
      "worked_solution": "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=6{x}^{x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6{x}^{x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡6+\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡6+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡6+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & ={(\\operatorname{ln}⁡6)}^{′}+(x)^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+(x)\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =0+1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡x+x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=6{x}^{x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =6{x}^{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
