    "q-341711": {
      "dom_id": "step-q341711",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Given that $y=e{x}^{2-5{x}^{2}}$, find ${y}^{′}.$\n\n${y}^{′}=e{x}^{1-5{x}^{2}}\\cdot ($\n{{field-1}}$)$",
      "worked_solution": "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=e{x}^{2-5{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e{x}^{2-5{x}^{2}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2-5{x}^{2}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =1+(2-5{x}^{2})\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1+(2-5{x}^{2})\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =0+(2-5{x}^{2})^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+(2-5{x}^{2})\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =(-10x)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡x+(2-5{x}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =-10x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{2-5{x}^{2}}{x} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =-10x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-5x+\\frac{2}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(-10x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-5x+\\frac{2}{x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=e{x}^{2-5{x}^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =e{x}^{2-5{x}^{2}}\\cdot (-10x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-5x+\\frac{2}{x}) \\\\  & =e{x}^{1-5{x}^{2}}\\cdot x(-10x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-5x+\\frac{2}{x}) \\\\  & =e{x}^{1-5{x}^{2}}\\cdot (-10{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-5{x}^{2}+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
