    "q-2519": {
      "dom_id": "step-q2519",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "Consider the function $y=(2x)^{x}.$ Its derivative is given by \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(2x)^{x}\\cdot g(x).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(x).$",
      "worked_solution": "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=(2x)^{x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((2x)^{x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =(x)^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x))+(x)\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x))}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2x}\\cdot 2) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=y=(2x)^{x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(2x)^{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+1.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
