    "q-138856": {
      "dom_id": "step-q138856",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "Consider the function $y=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{5x}.$ Its derivative is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=5(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{5x}\\cdot g(x).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(x).$",
      "worked_solution": "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{5x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{5x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =5x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =(5x)^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x))+(5x)\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x))}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =5\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+5x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-5x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =5(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(5(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{5x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =5(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{5x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
