    "q-341951": {
      "dom_id": "step-q341951",
      "name": "Question 14",
      "problem": "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent line to the curve $y=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{{x}^{2}}$ at $x=\\frac{e}{3}.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "The slope of the tangent to the curve at $x=\\frac{e}{3}$ equals the function's derivative at the corresponding point.\n\nWe have the function \n\n$$\ny=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{{x}^{2}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & ={x}^{2}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =({x}^{2})^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)))+({x}^{2})\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)))}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =2x\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))+{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))+\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))+\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{{x}^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{{x}^{2}}(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))+\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of $y=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{{x}^{2}}$ at $x=\\frac{e}{3}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={y}^{′}{|}_{x=e/3} & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e))^{(e/3)^{2}}(2(\\frac{e}{3})\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e))+\\frac{(\\frac{e}{3})}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e)}) \\\\  & =(1)^{(e/3)^{2}}(2(\\frac{e}{3})\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+\\frac{(\\frac{e}{3})}{1}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (0+\\frac{e}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
