      "name": "Question 15",
      "problem": "Find the slope of the tangent to the curve $y=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{5x}$ at $x=e.$",
      "worked_solution": "The slope of the tangent to the curve at $x=e$ equals the function's derivative at the corresponding point.\n\nWe have the function \n\n$$\ny=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{5x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{5x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =5x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =(5x)^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x))+(5x)\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x))}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =5\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+5x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\frac{5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =5(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(5(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{5x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =5(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{5x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of $y=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{5x}$ at $x=e$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}{|}_{x=e} & =5(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e)^{5\\cdot e}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e)+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡e}) \\\\  & =5(1)^{5e}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+\\frac{1}{1}) \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
